1.3  Related Work

Ontology development has a well-established methodology [14], but recent research at the intersection of ontologies and LLMs proceeds primarily in the opposite direction from our approach. DRAGON-AI uses retrieval-augmented LLM generation to construct biomedical ontologies from text corpora [15], and OLLM demonstrates end-to-end ontology learning with language models. These systems use LLMs to generate ontologies; we use ontologies to guide generation.

Mehenni and Zouaq [12] demonstrate ontology-constrained generation for clinical summaries, the closest methodological precedent to our work. However, their goal is hallucination reduction in summarization, whereas we target structured creativity. In game generation, Gallotta et al. [9] survey LLM applications in games broadly, while Becker et al. [4] present Boardwalk, a framework for generating board game code from existing rules, generating implementations, not designs. Maleki and Zhao [11] survey procedural content generation methods, noting emerging LLM integration but without ontological constraints. Cooper [7] provides foundational work on constrained decoding that underpins our structured output approach.

Our contribution fills a specific gap: using ontology as a generative grammar for structured creative output, operationalized through executable schemas, multi-agent pipelines, and retrieval augmentation.
