6.1  Two-Phase Retrieval

BoardGameGeek [1] contains data on over 100,000 tabletop games. We structure this corpus according to our ontology and employ a two-phase retrieval strategy: (1) ontology filtering, which narrows candidates to games sharing target mechanisms from the MechanismType taxonomy; then (2) semantic ranking, which ranks the filtered set by embedding similarity to the thematic query. This ensures retrieved games are both mechanically relevant and thematically resonant.

As a concrete example, for the prompt “trading empires in the ancient Mediterranean” with target mechanisms [TRADING, AREA_CONTROL, RESOURCE_MANAGEMENT], the system retrieves Concordia (card-driven trading networks across the Roman Empire), Cyclades (Greek island control with auction-based resource competition), and Tigris & Euphrates (civilization building with multi-dimensional area control scoring). The generated design synthesizes insights from Concordia’s card mechanism and Tigris & Euphrates’ balanced scoring into something novel that explicitly acknowledges its lineage in a design rationale field.
6.2  Grounded Creativity

RAG transforms Generative Ontology from invention to informed invention. The system knows what mechanisms have been successfully combined, how similar themes have been approached, and what design patterns work for given player counts and complexity levels. When retrieval returns few results for a mechanism–theme combination, we have discovered an unexplored design space. The absence of precedent is itself information. The ontology provides the grammar; the retrieved examples provide the vocabulary. Together, they enable generation that speaks the language of game design fluently.
7  Ontology as Validation Contract

Generation is not enough. LLMs are notoriously agreeable: they produce output that looks valid without ensuring it is valid. The ontology serves not only as a generation schema but as a validation contract: a set of constraints that generated designs must satisfy to count as coherent.
7.1  The Yes-Man Problem

An LLM asked to generate a deck-building game may mention deck_building as a mechanism yet include no cards in the components, producing structurally incoherent output that sounds like a deck-building game without being one. Pydantic validation catches type errors and missing fields, but semantic coherence (the relationship between mechanisms and components) requires deeper validation.
