7.3  Iterative Refinement

DSPy’s Assert mechanism enables inline validation: when dspy.Assert(is_valid, error_message) fails, DSPy automatically retries generation with the error message included as feedback. For complex validation requiring multiple attempts, we implement an explicit refinement loop: generate, validate, refine with specific issue descriptions, and repeat up to a configurable maximum. The validator returns both a boolean and a list of specific issues, enabling targeted correction rather than wholesale regeneration.

This architecture treats the ontology as a contract between the generation system and downstream consumers. Downstream systems (whether human designers reviewing output, automated balancing tools, or game engines instantiating the design) can rely on structural guarantees: a deck-building game will have cards; a cooperative game will not have direct conflict; the end condition will be specified.
8  Case Study: Mycelium: The Deep

Theory is persuasive; demonstration is convincing. We trace a complete generation through the Generative Ontology pipeline, from initial theme to playable game design.
8.1  Input

We provide a theme chosen to be unusual enough that retrieval finds no direct precedents, forcing the system to synthesize rather than imitate:

    Theme: “Bioluminescent fungi competing for dominance in a deep cave ecosystem”
    Constraints: 2–4 players, medium complexity, cooperative, 60–90 minutes

8.2  Phase 1: Mechanics Architect

The Mechanics Architect analyzes the theme and selects three core mechanisms: RESOURCE_MANAGEMENT (spore allocation across growth opportunities), AREA_CONTROL (territory expansion through the cave network), and ENGINE_BUILDING (specialized fungal structures providing ongoing benefits). The turn structure uses simultaneous planning (players place spore tokens secretly) followed by sequential resolution. The agency justification notes that players must balance short-term growth against long-term network health, with engine choices creating divergent strategies even in cooperative play.
8.3  Phase 2: Theme Weaver

The Theme Weaver integrates narrative, titling the game Mycelium: The Deep. The setting is an ancient limestone cave system where bioluminescent fungi are the only light source, slowly flooding as underground rivers shift. Thematic integration maps each mechanism to its fictional counterpart: spore placement is reproductive strategy; area control is securing nutrient pathways; engine building is developing specialized structures (fruiting bodies for points, rhizomorphs for fast movement, symbiotic bacteria for defense). The antagonist is the rising water table, creating cooperative tension without player-versus-player conflict.
8.4  Phase 3: Component Designer

The Component Designer specifies 30 modular hex cave tiles arranged vertically (deeper at the bottom), three card types (Event, Structure, Objective), example cards (“Flash Flood: Water rises 2 levels this round”; “Fruiting Body: Costs 3 growth, produces 1 VP per round”), and tokens including fungal growth cubes (15 per player in 4 colors), spore tokens (3 per player, regenerating each round), and flood markers (20 blue cubes advancing upward).
